      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "dd9d931e-2a0f-52b2-a5f5-940ff95dd292",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $M$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(M)=0$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & - \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & - \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{2}{5})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\sqrt{3})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & - \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}+\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{15})+(- \\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{2}{3}- \\frac{20}{3})+(- \\sqrt{3})(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{15}+\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{15}+(- \\frac{2}{5})\\cdot (-6)+(- \\sqrt{3})\\cdot \\frac{13 \\sqrt{3}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{12}{5}- \\frac{13}{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
