    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0b6e5ae2-3cd0-54a9-97e4-ad4f1817308b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $P$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $P$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(P)=2 \\sqrt{7}$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{1}{5} & - 2 \\sqrt{7} \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2} & - \\frac{3}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 \\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{1}{5} & - 2 \\sqrt{7} \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2} & - \\frac{3}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{7}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{7} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{5}|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7} & - \\sqrt{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+(- 2 \\sqrt{7})|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7} & - \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2} & - \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{7}(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5})+\\frac{1}{5}(- \\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{7}{2})+(- 2 \\sqrt{7})(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5}+\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}) \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{7}\\cdot (- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{5})+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0+(- 2 \\sqrt{7})\\cdot (- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{7}}{5}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{28}{5}+\\frac{28}{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
