  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "47babca6-773f-52a9-800d-9c009f7fee15",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $M$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(M)=\\frac{8}{27}$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{4}{3})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{9}+\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{9})+(- \\frac{4}{3})(\\frac{4}{9}- \\frac{4}{9})+(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{9}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{9}+(- \\frac{4}{3})\\cdot 0+(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})\\cdot (- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{27}+\\frac{4}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=\\frac{8}{27}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
